WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.847 --> 00:00:04.070
- [Narrator] Without Abraham
Lincoln, and really his beard,

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.870
Milton Bradley, the
godfather of board games

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.937
would have never existed.

00:00:08.937 --> 00:00:11.465
Bold statement, I know,
but let me explain.

00:00:11.465 --> 00:00:14.048
(upbeat music)

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:16.676
The Game of Life.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.746
The spinner, the cars, the choices,

00:00:20.746 --> 00:00:24.768
college or career, kids or
no kids, lawyer or farmer.

00:00:24.768 --> 00:00:28.778
This family game night staple
was once a pretty morbid game.

00:00:28.778 --> 00:00:31.431
Back in the 1800s, Milton Bradley was

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:33.476
in the lithograph business.

00:00:33.476 --> 00:00:36.493
Following the Republican
National Convention of 1860,

00:00:36.493 --> 00:00:38.986
Bradley printed thousands
of images of Abraham Lincoln

00:00:38.986 --> 00:00:41.359
who was clean-shaven at the time.

00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:44.813
Shortly thereafter, Lincoln
debuted his iconic beard,

00:00:44.813 --> 00:00:48.480
rendering all of Bradley's
prints worthless.

00:00:49.573 --> 00:00:52.740
His lithograph business went belly up.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.421
So, Bradley was forced
to try something new.

00:00:57.421 --> 00:00:59.900
He came up with a board
game, a seemingly dark

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.413
and twisted board game,
appropriately named

00:01:02.413 --> 00:01:04.447
The Checkered Game of Life.

00:01:04.447 --> 00:01:07.213
The game functioned in a
similar way to how it does now.

00:01:07.213 --> 00:01:09.512
There was a spinner,
colored circles that moved

00:01:09.512 --> 00:01:11.718
around the board, and of course squares

00:01:11.718 --> 00:01:13.925
that could either make you or break you.

00:01:13.925 --> 00:01:17.030
The squares on the original
game were overwhelmingly grim,

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.523
boasting actions like disgrace, poverty,

00:01:19.523 --> 00:01:23.363
ruin, crime, prison, and well, suicide.

00:01:23.363 --> 00:01:27.345
Regardless, the game flew off the shelves.

00:01:27.345 --> 00:01:29.472
Kids loved it and Milton Bradley went on

00:01:29.472 --> 00:01:31.173
to own family game night.

00:01:31.173 --> 00:01:33.092
Fast forward to about
a hundred years later.

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:35.781
They revamped the game, trading
Bradley's morbid squares

00:01:35.781 --> 00:01:39.435
for the more delightful ones
like payday, or graduation,

00:01:39.435 --> 00:01:41.722
which we have all come to know and love.

00:01:41.722 --> 00:01:45.082
And so there you have it, the
story of the game called Life.

00:01:45.082 --> 00:01:46.571
Thanks, Lincoln.

00:01:46.571 --> 00:01:49.154
(bright music)

